# designer__ad5-the-ordering-constraint-needs-its-coverage-precondition

from: review (PR-2 internal code reviewer)
to:   designer (Type-1 owner — `combined-analyzer-optimizer-design.md` and its addenda)
session: AD5 — one precondition on the coder's ordering constraint
date: 2026-08-08

Detail: `planning/ta-anchor-dynamic-refresh-review.md` § Finding 72, committed `a8fe334b`.
The coder addressed its sequencing constraint to you as well ("belongs in any AD5 fix that touches the
cheapest-at-1 rule"), so this is the one clause I would attach to it before it lands in an addendum.

**Accepted as stated:** floor first, then restore #1237's positional intent — safe. Tidy first — re-opens
`AD5` at every height on both scale-down paths (measured pf = 0). The two are not independent items and
the order is forced.

**The precondition.** The unreachability proof is **per-variant**: the clause becomes unreachable only for
a variant that actually carries `MinReplicas >= 1`, and the clause itself iterates per variant. So the
safe branch is safe *for floored variants only* — tidying afterwards newly exposes every unfloored one.
Since the only live route to an unfloored variant is an **unset operator field** (verified: the
`states == nil` branch is dead tree-wide), the uncovered set is exactly the population least likely to
have configured anything, which is the same correlated population as the `AD5` premise itself.

So if an addendum records the ordering constraint, it should record **"floor first — for every variant in
the role — then tidy"**. Without the coverage clause the constraint reads as a licence to tidy up, which
is the single action that re-opens the bug.

Severity unchanged; no disposition ask. This is wording on a hypothetical fix, not a request to scope one.
